       <h1>Exercise Name:-  (Close-Grip Underhand Lat Pulldown)</h1>
        <u><b><h4>Exercise Overview</h4></b></u>

<pre>This 3D anatomical visualization demonstrates a Close Underhand Lat Pulldown (Reverse-Grip Lat Pulldown)
     viewed from behind. The video highlights the lower lats and upper back muscles (Latissimus Dorsi and 
     Rhomboids) in glowing orange and blue, illustrating how taking a close supinated (underhand) grip drives
      the elbows downward and back to deeply contract the latissimus dorsi.

 Exercise Name: Close-Grip Underhand Lat Pulldown (Reverse-Grip Lat Pulldown)
 Target Muscles: Lower Lats (Latissimus Dorsi — lower fibers), Middle Back (Rhomboids, Lower Trapezius)
 Secondary Muscles: Biceps (Biceps Brachii), Brachialis, Forearms
</pre>
</div></div>


    <h1>
        
        How to Perform (Step-by-Step)</h1> <u><b><h4>1. Setup</h4></b></u>Setup: Adjust the thigh pad on the lat pulldown machine so your legs are firmly locked in place. Reach up and grip the straight bar with an underhand grip
         (palms facing toward you) about shoulder-width apart.
 <u><b><h4>2. Positioning</h4></b></u>: Sit down with your feet flat on the floor. Keep your torso upright with a slight natural arch in your lower back and your chest lifted up toward the bar.
 <u><b><h4>3. Pulling Phase (Concentric)</h4></b></u>: Exhale as you pull the bar straight down toward your upper chest. Focus on driving your elbows close to your sides and down toward your lower back.
 <u><b><h4>4. Peak Squeeze</h4></b></u>: Pause for a split second at the bottom position when the bar reaches upper chest level, squeezing your lower lats and back together tightly.
 <u><b><h4>5. Return Phase (Eccentric)</h4></b></u>: Inhale as you slowly extend your arms back upward over 2 to 3 seconds until your lats are fully stretched at the top.



<h1>Key Benefits</h1>

 <b>◉Greater Range of Motion:</b> The underhand grip allows for increased shoulder extension, placing a deep stretch and strong contraction on the lower lats.<br>
 
 <b>◉High Bicep Leverage:</b> Underhand grip position allows your biceps to assist more heavily than wide overhand grips, making it easier to handle heavier weight safely.<br>
 
 <b>◉Shoulder-Friendly Path:</b> Keeps your elbows tucked close to your torso, reducing impingement stress on the shoulder joints.


<h1>Recommended Sets & Reps</h1>

| Goal | Sets | Reps | Rest |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Muscle Building (Hypertrophy) | 3 – 4 | 10 – 12 | 60 – 90 seconds |
| Endurance / Tone | 3 – 4 | 12 – 15 | 45 – 60 seconds |
| Strength | 4 – 5 | 6 – 8 | 2 – 3 minutes |

Avoid leaning back excessively or using momentum to jerk the bar down—keep your torso stable and let your back and biceps do all the work.

